---Used to represent positions, taking into account absolute horizontal and vertical offsets, as well as a specific
---percent of an arbitrary parent component's width and height, and a component's own width and height.
---
---Since position objects just store offsets and percentages, it's only when associated with a component that they can
---be used to calculate a final position value.
---
---Info: The resulting position is calculated as `position = x/y + xParentPc/yParentPc * <parentWidth>/<parentHeight> +
---xSelfPc/ySelfPc * <selfWidth>/<selfHeight>`
---@class ui.Pos
---@field x integer Horizontal offset in interface units.
---@field y integer Vertical offset in interface units.
---@field xParentPc number Percentage of the parent component's width to take into account for positioning.
---@field yParentPc number Percentage of the parent component's height to take into account for positioning.
---@field xSelfPc number Percentage of the component's width to take into account for positioning.
---@field ySelfPc number Percentage of the component's height to take into account for positioning.
ui.Pos = {}

---Creates a new position object.
---@return ui.Pos # The newly created position object.
---@overload fun(other: ui.Pos): ui.Pos
---@overload fun(x: integer, y: integer): ui.Pos
---@overload fun(x: integer, y: integer, xParentPc: number, yParentPc: number): ui.Pos
---@overload fun(x: integer, y: integer, xParentPc: number, yParentPc: number, xSelfPc: number, ySelfPc: number): ui.Pos
function ui.Pos.new() end

---Used to represent sizes, taking into account absolute horizontal and vertical offsets, as well as a specific percent
---of an arbitrary parent component's width and height, as well of a component's own width and height.
---
---Since size objects just store offsets and percentages, it's only when associated with a component that they can be
---used to calculate a final size value.
---
---Info: The resulting size is calculated as `size = width/height + widthParentPc/heightParentPc *
---<parentWidth>/<parentHeight>`
---@class ui.Size
---@field width integer Width offset in interface units.
---@field height integer Height offset in interface units.
---@field widthParentPc number Percentage of the parent component's width to take into account for sizing.
---@field heightParentPc number Percentage of the parent component's height to take into account for sizing.
ui.Size = {}

---Creates a new position object.
---@return ui.Size # The newly created size object.
---@overload fun(other: ui.Size): ui.Size
---@overload fun(width: integer, height: integer): ui.Size
---@overload fun(width: integer, height: integer, widthParentPc: number, heightParentPc: number): ui.Size
function ui.Size.new() end

---Used to represent a text element within a UI component. These contain properties necessary for drawing text, as well
---as the actual text to draw.
---
---ui.TextDataConfig: TextDataConfigs are simplified versions of TextData, containing only the properties necessary for
---drawing text, allowing them to be initialised separately and shared across components.
---
---Essentially they contain all of the same properties and data, except the `content` property is omitted and no methods
---are available.
---@class ui.TextData
---@field content string The text to draw.
---@field font integer The font to use when drawing this text.
---@field lineSpacing integer Spacing between text lines in interface units. Defaults to the font's spacing if set to zero.
---@field alignHorizontal ui.AlignMode Horizontal alignment for this text.
---@field alignVertical ui.AlignMode Vertical alignment for this text.
---@field maxLines integer Maximum number of lines to display. Can be set to 0 to allow an unlimited number of lines.
---@field alpha number Visibility for this text, ranging from 0 (fully transparent) to 1 (opaque).
---@field colour Vector4 Colour used when drawing this text.
---@field rgba Long Hex-encoded red/green/blue/alpha colour code used when drawing this text.
---@field shadowColour Vector4 Colour used when drawing this text's shadow if enabled.
---@field shadowRGBA Long Hex-encoded red/green/blue/alpha colour code used when drawing this text's shadow if enabled.
---@field isShadowed boolean Whether or not to draw this text with a shadow effect for better legibility. Defaults to false.
local TextData = {}

---Sets the alignment for this text.
---@param alignment ui.AlignMode The alignment to set as both horizontal and vertical alignments.
---@overload fun(self: ui.TextData, horizontalAlignment: ui.AlignMode, verticalAlignment: ui.AlignMode)
function TextData:AlignTo(alignment) end

---Sets this TextData's properties based on the provided config values.
---@param config ui.TextDataConfig The config to source the various properties from.
function TextData:SetConfig(config) end

---Returns a copy of this TextData's config, allowing it to be used elsewhere.
---@return ui.TextDataConfig # Config containing a copy of all properties set on this TextData object, excluding non-config properties (eg. `content`)
function TextData:GetConfigCopy() end

---Used to represent a sprite element within a UI component. These contain properties necessary for drawing sprite, as
---well as the actual sprite resource to draw.
---
---Sprite resources can contained tiled visuals, or be split into multiple segments, up to a 3x3 segmented sprite,
---containing segments for the four sides, the 4 corners, and the centre region.
---
---Info: Unless they're tiled, sprite visuals will be stretched to occupy the region provided by the component
---containing that sprite. This is particularly important when using segmented sprites, since for a 3x3 sprite the
---corners don't get stretched at all, the edges get stretched in one direction only (for example, left and right edges
---stretch vertically), and the centre segment stretches as normal.
---
---Info: Prefer using segmented sprites and tiling for sprites used for functional elements such as buttons, and
---limiting non-tiled and non-segmented sprites to decorative elements.
---
---ui.SpriteDataConfig: SpriteDataConfigs are simplified versions of SpriteData, containing only the properties
---necessary for drawing a sprite, allowing them to be initialised separately and shared across components.
---
---Essentially they contain all of the same properties and data, except the `sprite`, `spriteID`, and `runtimeSprite`
---properties are omitted and no methods are available.
---
---RuntimeSprite: An opaque object representing a sprite generated at runtime.
---
---These are generally sprites representing objects associated with the containing components, such as those used for
---inventory icons.
---
---Image Layout: Sprites can be split into segments via the `visualMargin` property.
---
---For example by specifying left and right margins you obtain a 3x1 segments sprite, with the left and right segments
---having a fixed width and stretching vertically, while the middle one stretches horizontally and vertically:
---
---While by specifying top and bottom margins you obtain a 1x3 segments sprite, with the top and bottom segments having
---a fixed height and stretch horizontally, while the middle one stretches horizontally and vertically:
---
---Or you can specify all margins, resulting in a 3x3 segments sprite, where the corners don't stretch at all, and the
---edges stretch only in one direction, while the middle segment stretches horizontally and vertically:
---@class ui.SpriteData
---@field sprite integer|RuntimeSprite|PluginImageResource
---@field spriteID integer Gets the built-in sprite ID to draw.
---@field runtimeSprite RuntimeSprite? Gets the current runtime sprite to draw or nil if it doesn't exist.
---@field visualMargins ui.Margin (Modifiable Contents) Offsets used to split this sprite's graphics into multiple segments for drawing. See image layout for information on how they're used.
---@field outlineWidth integer Thickness of the sprite's outline. Only used when the containing component is associated with an object.
---@field rotationRadians number Rotation of this sprite in radians.
---@field rotationDegrees number Rotation of this sprite in degrees.
---@field alpha number Visibility for this sprite, ranging from 0 (fully transparent) to 1 (opaque).
---@field colour Vector4 Colour used when drawing this sprite.
---@field rgba Long Hex-encoded red/green/blue/alpha colour code used when drawing this sprite.
---@field shadowColour Vector4 Colour used when drawing this sprite's shadow. If alpha is 0, no shadow will be drawn.
---@field shadowRGBA Long Hex-encoded red/green/blue/alpha colour code used when drawing this sprite. If alpha is 0, no shadow will be drawn.
---@field isFlippedHorizontally boolean Flag indicating whether or not this sprite is drawn mirrored horizontally.
---@field isFlippedVertical boolean Flag indicating whether or not this sprite is drawn mirrored vertical.
---@field isTiling boolean Flag indicating whether or not this sprite uses tiled visuals.
local SpriteData = {}

---Sets this SpriteData's properties based on the provided config values.
---@param config ui.SpriteDataConfig The config to source the various properties from.
function SpriteData:SetConfig(config) end

---Returns a copy of this SpriteData's config, allowing it to be used elsewhere.
---@return ui.SpriteDataConfig # Config containing a copy of all properties set on this SpriteData object, excluding non-config properties (eg. `content`)
function SpriteData:GetConfigCopy() end

---Margins are used to represent distances from the sides of a region.
---
---They're commonly used when defining slices for 3x3 sprites, or specifying the content regions for UI components.
---@class ui.Margin
---@field left integer Distance from the left edge in interface units for this margin.
---@field top integer Distance from the top edge in interface units for this margin.
---@field right integer Distance from the right edge in interface units for this margin.
---@field bottom integer Distance from the bottom edge in interface units for this margin.
ui.Margin = {}

---Constructs a new margin object with the provided values, or with all sides initialised to zero if omitted.
---@return ui.Margin # Resulting margin object.
---@overload fun(allSides: integer): ui.Margin
---@overload fun(left: integer, top: integer, right: integer, bottom: integer): ui.Margin
function ui.Margin.new() end

---Provides controls for scrolling a component's contents.
---
---Info: Custom sprites are supported, however note that for `handleSprite` and `backgroundSprite`, you must supply a
---sprite containing the following visual states in a vertical layout - `default/idle`, `mouseover`, `pressed`,
---`disabled`.
---@class ui.ScrollBar
---@field size integer The scrollbar's size, representing width or height depending on whether it's a vertical or a horizontal scrollbar, respectively.
---@field visible boolean Controls whether the scrollbar is visible or not. Defaults to true.
---@field autohide boolean Controls whether the scrollbar only appears to the user when required by the component where the scrollbar resides. For example, an input field may hide the scrollbar until the contents exceed the input field's visible region. Note this doesn't affect the value of `visible`, as that controls whether the scrollbar can ever appear to the user. Defaults to true.
---@field buttonLength integer The length of the scrollbar's buttons, representing height or width depending on whether it's a vertical or a horizontal scrollbar. Can be set to `0` to remove the buttons.
---@field backgroundSprite ui.SpriteData (Modifiable Contents) Sprite properties used for the background.
---@field buttonSprite ui.SpriteData (Modifiable Contents) Sprite properties used for the scrollbar's buttons.
---@field handleSprite ui.SpriteData (Modifiable Contents) Sprite properties used for the scrollbar's draggable handle.

